-- citrate and aconitase: the inference the construction refutes, made in 1948 and refuted the same year.
--
-- Wood and colleagues introduced isotopic carbon as carbon dioxide together with pyruvate and found
-- ketoglutarate carrying the label only in the carboxy group next to the keto group. On those grounds they
-- excluded citrate as an intermediate of the cycle: citrate is symmetric, the product is not, so the
-- intermediate cannot have been citrate. Ogston pointed out that the inference does not hold (Nature 162,
-- 963, 18 December 1948): an asymmetric enzyme attacking a symmetric compound can distinguish between its
-- identical groups, so the label's position says nothing against a symmetric intermediate. Aconitase acts
-- on the arm of citrate derived from oxaloacetate, every time, and the three-point attachment is how it
-- tells the arms apart.
--
-- The inference is the one this whole section is about: it treats an observation of a molecule in an
-- environment as a function of the molecule alone. The environment here is an enzyme, which is chiral, and
-- the arms of citrate are enantiotopic -- the same relation as glycerol's arms and the same as the
-- methylene hydrogens Pirkle's alcohol separates. What follows is the formal statement of why the
-- inference fails, with the two molecules it cannot tell apart.
--
-- citrate: its one operation is the exchange of the two carboxymethyl arms, which is improper
